## 2. Users, roles and access management

| Role                                | Can do                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              | Cannot do                                                                                                                                                                                                   |
|-------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| **Admin / Owner** — Muhammad Wakeel | Everything: create and edit projects, set and revise contract values, record client payments and schedules, add / edit / delete expenses, manage users and project assignments, view margins and all reports, restore deleted records, export data. | Permanently erase data (all deletes are soft — see §7). Edit the audit log.                                                                                                                                 |
| **Site Manager** — Site supervisor  | Add expenses to projects assigned to him. View the expense list and daily / monthly expense totals for his own assigned projects only. Edit an expense he created, within 24 hours of creating it (window configurable by admin).                   | Delete anything. See contract value, client payments, payment schedule, amount receivable, cash in hand, margin, or any company-wide figures. See projects he is not assigned to. Create projects or users. |

There is no public signup. The admin invites users by email; Supabase Auth sends the invite.

*Reserved for later (do not build in v1):* an **Accountant** role with read-only access to everything, and a **Client** role with read-only access to their own project statement.

### 2.1 Inviting users (admin only)

The team will grow — today one site supervisor, more later. The admin must be able to add users himself, without a developer. From Settings → Users:

- **Add user** form: full name, role, and either a **mobile number** or an **email address** as the login identifier (at least one required; both allowed)
- The app generates a **temporary password** automatically — 8 characters, readable, no ambiguous characters (exclude O/0, I/l/1)
- Optionally assign projects during creation, or leave for later
- On save, show the credentials **once** on screen in a clear panel: login identifier + temporary password, with a **Copy** button and a **Share on WhatsApp** button (a `wa.me` deep link pre-filled with the credentials message). This is how they will actually be delivered in practice. Note this is a plain `wa.me` link that opens WhatsApp on the admin's own phone — it is **not** a WhatsApp API integration and costs nothing.
- If an email was provided, also send the credentials by email automatically
- Admin can **reset password** for any user at any time, which generates a fresh temporary password shown the same way
- Admin can **deactivate** a user (blocks login, keeps all their historical entries intact) and **reactivate** later. Users are never deleted.

### 2.2 First login and password change

- The user logs in with their phone number or email plus the temporary password
- `must_change_password` is set true on creation and after any admin reset. While it is true, the user is routed to a Set Your Password screen and cannot reach any other part of the app
- Minimum 8 characters, confirmed twice; once set, the flag clears and normal routing resumes
- Afterwards the user can change their own password any time from Profile → Change password
- Temporary passwords expire after 7 days; an expired one requires an admin reset

> **Implementation note on phone-number login.** Supabase Auth supports phone login only through SMS OTP, which needs a paid SMS provider (Twilio or similar) and is unreliable and costly for Pakistani numbers. Do **not** build it that way. Instead keep Supabase's standard email/password auth and map phone numbers onto it: when the admin enters a mobile number, normalise it to `+92XXXXXXXXXX` and create the auth user with a synthetic internal address such as `923001234567@users.siteledger.app`, storing the real phone in `profiles.phone`. The login screen accepts a phone number or an email; if the input looks like a phone number, the app normalises it and swaps in the synthetic address before calling Supabase. The user only ever sees and types their phone number. This gives phone-based login with no SMS cost and no OTP dependency.

### 2.3 Assigning projects to users

- A site manager sees only the projects assigned to him — no assignment means no visibility, enforced by RLS through `project_assignments`
- Assignment is editable from two directions: on the user (a multi-select of projects) and on the project (a multi-select of users). Both write the same table.
- One user may hold many projects; one project may have several users
- Removing an assignment hides the project from that user going forward but **never** removes or hides the expenses he already entered — those stay on the project permanently, attributed to him
- Admins are implicitly assigned to everything and need no rows in this table

### 2.4 No limits on accounts or projects

There is no cap anywhere in the system: any number of admins, any number of site managers, any number of projects. Do not build seat limits, plan tiers, or a hardcoded single-admin assumption. Several people may hold the admin role simultaneously and all have identical powers.

Expect 5 to 30 active projects at a time and several hundred completed ones over the years, with tens of thousands of expense rows. Every list, dashboard figure and filter must be built for that scale rather than assuming one project — paginate lists, index the tables per §11, and compute dashboard totals with database aggregates rather than by pulling all rows into the browser.

### 2.5 Activating and deactivating users

All of this is done by an admin from the normal application interface. No developer involvement and no database access is ever required to manage people.

- **Deactivate** — blocks login immediately and removes the user from assignment dropdowns. Every expense they entered stays exactly where it is, still attributed to them. Nothing is hidden or removed.
- **Reactivate** — restores login with the same account and history. If the password was forgotten in the meantime, the admin issues a fresh temporary password per §2.1.
- Deactivated users appear in a separate collapsed section of the Users screen, not mixed in with active ones.
- **Users are never deleted.** Deletion would orphan historical expense records and break the audit trail. Deactivation is the only removal mechanism, and the Users screen should not offer a delete action at all.
- An admin cannot deactivate their own account, and the last remaining active admin cannot be deactivated — block it with a clear message.

