## 4. Core objects

### 4.1 Project

- Project name (e.g. "Ahmed House — Bahria Phase 8")
- Client name, client phone (+92 format)
- Site address / area; plot size in marla or kanal (optional); covered area in sq ft (optional)
- Project type (per §3); supervision % if cost-plus
- Contract value (PKR)
- Start date, expected completion date
- Status: Active / On hold / Completed / Archived
- Assigned site manager(s)
- Notes

### 4.2 Payment milestone (the pre-agreed payment schedule)

Clients pay in chunks against an agreed schedule, so the schedule is a first-class object, not a note.

- Milestone name — seed suggestions: Advance / Booking, Foundation, DPC, Roof slab (first), Roof slab (second), Grey structure complete, Brickwork, Plaster, Electrical & plumbing, Finishing, Handover
- Planned amount (PKR), planned date *or* "on stage completion"
- Sequence number (for ordering)
- Derived status: Pending / Partially received / Received / Overdue (overdue = planned date passed and not fully received)

The sum of milestone amounts should be compared to the contract value; if they do not match, show a soft warning to the admin (do not block — schedules change).

### 4.3 Client payment (money in)

- Project, date received, amount
- Method: Cash / Bank transfer / Cheque / Easypaisa / JazzCash / Other
- Reference number (cheque no., transaction ID) — optional
- Linked milestone — optional; one payment may partly cover a milestone
- Received by, note

### 4.4 Expense (money out) — the most-used object in the app

| Field                      | Required | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |
|----------------------------|----------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| Project                    | Yes      | Site manager sees only his assigned projects in the dropdown.                                                                                                                                                                     |
| Expense name / description | Yes      | Short. e.g. "50 bags cement".                                                                                                                                                                                                     |
| Quantity                   | No       | Numeric. e.g. 100.                                                                                                                                                                                                                |
| Unit                       | No       | Free text with autocomplete: bag, kg, ton, foot, sq ft, trip, day, piece, bundle.                                                                                                                                                 |
| Rate (PKR)                 | No       | Price per unit. e.g. 850.                                                                                                                                                                                                         |
| Amount (PKR)               | Yes      | Auto-calculated as quantity × rate when both are filled, but always editable — the typed amount wins if the user overrides it. Accepts shorthand input — see §9. If quantity and rate are left blank, amount is entered directly. |
| Date of expense            | Yes      | Defaults to today (Asia/Karachi). Future dates blocked. Site manager may back-date up to 7 days; admin unrestricted.                                                                                                              |
| Category                   | Yes      | Free text with autocomplete from previously used values — see §8.                                                                                                                                                                 |
| Purpose / note             | No       | Why the expense was made. e.g. "for roof slab, block B".                                                                                                                                                                          |
| Payee / vendor             | No       | Free text with autocomplete. Drives the "who do I owe" view.                                                                                                                                                                      |
| Payment status             | Yes      | **Paid** or **Unpaid (due)**. If Paid: paid date + method.                                                                                                                                                                        |
| Paid via                   | Yes      | Who physically handed the money over: **Site float** (from cash the site manager is holding — see §4.8) or **Direct** (paid by the admin himself). Defaults to Site float for site managers, Direct for admins.                   |
| Entered by / entered at    | Auto     | Set server-side from the session. Never editable.                                                                                                                                                                                 |

### 4.5 Vendor / payee

Auto-created from the payee field, deduplicated on a normalised name (trimmed, case-insensitive). Gives a simple per-payee outstanding balance. Admin can merge duplicates.

### 4.6 Contract revision

Extra work and variations are normal. Contract value is never silently overwritten. Every change writes a row: date, old value, new value, reason, changed by. The project header shows "Revised" with a tooltip listing the history.

### 4.7 Cash issuance and the site float

The admin hands cash to a site manager, who then spends it over the following days. Without tracking this, the app cannot tell whether money has left the business or is simply sitting in someone's pocket.

- **Only an admin creates a cash issuance.** The site manager can never add money to his own float. Fields: date, recipient (a user), amount, method, note.
- **The float is one pocket per person, never tied to a project.** Cash issued to Ahsan is simply cash with Ahsan. He may spend it on any project assigned to him, and each expense draws down the same single balance. There is deliberately no project field on an issuance — see the box below.
- Each user has a running **float balance** = total issued to him − total spent from his float.
- Every expense marked *Paid via: Site float* draws the float of the user who entered it down automatically.
- The site manager sees his own float balance prominently on his dashboard and at the top of the Add Expense form: "Cash with you: Rs 32,500". This is the only money figure he ever sees, and it is his own cash custody, not client money.
- A negative float means he spent more than he was given — he is owed a reimbursement. Show it in red on both his screen and the admin's.
- **A float at zero never blocks an entry.** If his balance is exhausted and he adds another expense paid from the float, save it and let the balance go negative — he genuinely did spend that money and the record must exist. Show a non-blocking notice ("This takes your cash below zero. The company now owes you Rs X") and notify the admin. Blocking the entry would simply mean the expense goes unrecorded, which is the worst outcome.
- Admin sees a **Cash with staff** panel: every user, amount issued, amount spent, balance still held, and the date of the last issuance.
- Returning unspent cash is recorded as a **negative issuance** ("cash returned"), which reduces the float. Do not build a separate object for it.
- Issuances follow the same soft-delete and audit rules as everything else.

> **Why the float is not project-specific.** Physical cash in a man's pocket does not know which site it was meant for. If a float were tied to one project, the first time Ahsan was given money for Site A and spent it at Site B the app would either block a legitimate entry or quietly produce a wrong balance. Per-project attribution already comes from the *expense*, which always names its project, so nothing is lost: his ledger can be filtered by project to show exactly where the float went. Attribution comes from the expense; custody comes from the person. Do not add a project field to issuances, and do not validate an expense against which project the cash was "for".

> **There is no "whose money" field on an expense.** *Paid via* answers the only question that must be recorded by hand: does the site manager still have this cash in his pocket, or did the admin pay it directly? Whether the money originated from the client or from the owner's own pocket is **never tagged** — it is computed from cash flow per §5, because a tag can be set wrongly in a hurry and a calculation cannot.

### 4.8 Project lifecycle — archive, not delete

A project holds months of financial history. Archiving is the normal way to retire one; deleting is close to never.

- **Archive** is a first-class, prominent action available to any admin from the project detail screen and from the projects list. It sets status to Archived: the project drops out of the default list, out of dashboard totals, and out of every assigned user's view, but all its expenses, payments and documents remain fully intact and readable. **Unarchive** restores it completely. This action is fully reversible and needs no warning beyond a simple confirm.
- Archived projects live behind a filter or a separate tab, searchable at any time, with all their figures still viewable.
- **Delete** must exist but must be hard to reach. It belongs at the bottom of the project's Settings tab in a small, plainly-styled destructive section — never a visible button on the project header, the projects list, a row action menu, or a card. Reaching it requires: opening project settings, scrolling to the bottom, and typing the exact project name to confirm.
- Deleting a project is still a soft delete (§7): the project and everything under it move to Trash and remain fully recoverable by an admin. Nothing is erased from the database.
- Show a clear warning at that point stating what will be hidden — expense count, payment count, total value — and suggest archiving instead.
- Only an admin may archive or delete. Site managers see neither action.

