## 6. Screens

### 6.1 Login

Email + password (Supabase Auth), "forgot password" flow. No signup link. After login, route by role: admin → admin dashboard, site manager → site dashboard.

### 6.2 Admin dashboard

The whole financial position of the business on one screen, without scrolling past the numbers. These eight are the **primary numbers** and appear as cards at the top, each summed across all *active* projects:

| Card                       | Meaning                                                     |
|----------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------|
| **Spent today**            | All expenses dated today, across every project              |
| **Spent this month**       | All expenses in the current calendar month                  |
| **Clients have paid**      | Total received across active projects                       |
| **Clients still owe**      | Total receivable — the sum of every project's balance due   |
| **Client money with us**   | Client funds received but not yet spent                     |
| **Our own money in sites** | Sum of `spent_from_own` across all projects                 |
| **Cash with staff**        | Total float issued and not yet spent, held by site managers |
| **Still to pay vendors**   | Total unpaid expenses across all projects                   |

Below the cards:

- Active projects list — each row: project name, client, contract value, received, balance due, spent, budget progress bar
- Alerts panel: milestones overdue, projects above 80% budget, expenses unpaid over 30 days, staff floats running low or negative
- Quick actions: Add expense · Record client payment · Issue cash · New project

Every card is tappable and opens the list it summarises, already filtered. A period selector (Today / This month / This year / All time / custom range) re-scopes the cards where a period is meaningful.

### 6.3 Site manager dashboard

Deliberately minimal, and built around the one number that matters to him. His **primary numbers**, as cards at the top:

| Card                     | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                       |
|--------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| **Cash with you**        | The headline figure, largest on the screen: money the admin has given him that he has not yet spent. Negative means the business owes him — label it "Company owes you Rs X". |
| **You spent today**      | Total of his own entries dated today                                                                                                                                          |
| **You spent this month** | Total of his own entries this calendar month                                                                                                                                  |
| **Your projects**        | Count of projects assigned to him                                                                                                                                             |

Then a large **Add Expense** button, today's entries listed with a day total, and links into his assigned projects' expense lists and his own cash ledger (§6.12).

**Cash with you is the only money figure he ever sees.** No contract values, client payments, receivables, margins, company totals, or other users' figures on this screen or any screen he can reach.

### 6.4 Projects list (admin)

Table or cards, filterable by status and type, searchable by project or client name, sortable by balance due.

### 6.5 Project detail (admin)

Opening a project must answer the owner's questions **above the fold, before any tab or scroll**, in plain words and large type. Lay the header out as two rows of cards:

| Row 1 — the money    | Label shown           | Value                                                                             |
|----------------------|-----------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| What the client sent | **Client has paid**   | `total_received`, with contract value beneath it in small type: "of Rs 50,00,000" |
| What we spent        | **Total spent**       | `total_expenses`                                                                  |
| Still to collect     | **Client still owes** | `client_balance_due`                                                              |
| Profit position      | **Margin**            | `project_margin` with percentage                                                  |

| Row 2 — whose money   | Label shown                    | Value                                                                    |
|-----------------------|--------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| Spent from his money  | **Spent from client's money**  | `MIN(total_paid_out, total_received)`                                    |
| Spent from our pocket | **Spent from our own pocket**  | `MAX(0, total_paid_out - total_received)` — shown in red when above zero |
| Client money left     | **Client money still with us** | `MAX(0, total_received - total_paid_out)`                                |
| Unpaid bills          | **Still to pay vendors**       | `outstanding_payables`                                                   |

Row 2 is a specific requirement: at a glance, how much of the spending came out of the client's money and how much came out of the owner's own pocket. Two arithmetic rules hold and should be used as a self-check — **"Spent from client's money" plus "Spent from our own pocket" always equals `total_paid_out`**, and **"Client money still with us" and "Spent from our own pocket" can never both be above zero**. Either the client's money is exhausted and the owner is funding the site, or it is not. If either rule fails, the calculation is wrong.

> **These figures are computed, never tagged.** The "whose money" numbers come entirely from actual cash flow. There is no field on an expense recording whose money it was, and none should be added — a hand-set tag can be wrong, and arithmetic cannot.

On a phone these render as two rows of two cards; on desktop as one eight-card strip. Every figure uses lakh/crore shorthand per §9, and each card is tappable, jumping to the tab that explains it. Then tabs:

- **Overview** — the numbers above plus a spend-by-category donut and a spend-over-time line
- **Expenses** — filterable table (date range, category, payee, paid/unpaid), running total, group-by toggle (date / category / payee), inline edit, soft delete
- **Payments received** — list of client payments with running total received
- **Payment schedule** — milestones with planned vs received and status chips
- **Activity log** — who changed what and when, from the audit table

### 6.6 Add expense (mobile-first — the most important form)

Designed for a supervisor standing on a dusty site on an Android phone.

- Reachable in two taps from login
- Large touch targets, minimal typing, numeric keypad for amount
- Project preselected if the user has only one assignment
- Date defaults to today with a one-tap "yesterday" shortcut
- Category as tappable chips of recent/common values, with free text as a fallback
- Typed data must survive a dropped connection — keep form state and retry, never lose the entry
- Clear success confirmation, then offer "Add another"

### 6.7 Daily view

A single date with previous / next arrows and a date picker. Shows every expense on that date, grouped by category, with the day total, and a per-project breakdown when viewing all projects. Answers "what was spent today, and on what". Site manager sees the same view scoped to his own projects with no money-in data.

### 6.8 Monthly and yearly views

Month picker with previous / next arrows. Shows: month total, breakdown by project, breakdown by category, breakdown by person who entered it, a bar chart of daily spend across the month, and a comparison against the previous month.

A **year** toggle on the same screen switches the period to a calendar year or the last twelve months: year total, month-by-month bars, and the same breakdowns by project, category and person. The owner should be able to answer "what did we spend in the last year, and on which sites" without exporting anything.

### 6.9 Payables ("Who do I owe")

All unpaid expenses across all projects, grouped by payee, oldest first, with a per-payee total and a one-tap "Mark as paid" that records paid date and method.

### 6.10 Reports and export

- Date-range export to CSV for expenses and for client payments
- **Client statement PDF** — a clean one-page statement per project: contract value, payments received with dates, total spent (optional, toggle on/off before generating), balance due. Shareable on WhatsApp. This is a frequent real-world need.

### 6.11 Notifications

There is no approval workflow — entries count immediately. Instead the admin is notified as things happen, so he is never surprised by what appeared on a site.

#### Events that trigger a notification (admin only)

- A new expense is added by anyone other than the admin — payee, amount, project, category
- An expense above a configurable threshold is added (e.g. over Rs 50,000) — flagged as high value
- A client payment is recorded
- A site manager's float balance drops below a configurable level, or goes negative
- An expense is edited or deleted
- Optional end-of-day digest at a configurable time (default 9:00 PM PKT): total spent today, broken down by project, plus the day's entry count

Each event type has its own on/off toggle in settings, plus quiet hours during which notifications are held and batched until morning.

#### Delivery — mobile push only

Phone notifications through the installed PWA are the **only** channel in this build. Delivered with the Web Push API, using a Supabase Edge Function as the sender and VAPID keys. There is no WhatsApp integration, no SMS and no email alerting — deliberately, since the official WhatsApp Business API carries Meta verification, template approval and per-conversation billing that is not worth the cost here.

Build the sender behind a small channel-agnostic interface anyway, so a second channel can be added later as an adapter without touching the notification logic. Do not build any adapter other than push now.

> **Platform constraint worth knowing before you promise it.** On Android, web push works in Chrome without the app being installed. **On iPhone it only works if the web app has been added to the home screen** (iOS 16.4 and above) — Safari will not deliver push to a normal browser tab, ever. So the first-run experience must actively prompt the admin to install the app to his home screen, and the notification settings screen should detect and warn when push permission or installation is missing rather than silently failing.

#### Site manager notifications

He receives only: cash issued to him by the admin, and a low-float reminder. Nothing about other users, other projects, or any client money.

### 6.12 User detail — "cash with him right now"

From the Users screen or the admin dashboard's Cash with staff panel, tapping a person opens their page. Its purpose is to answer one question immediately: **how much of my cash is in this man's pocket at this moment?**

#### Header

- **Cash with him right now** — the largest figure on the page: `float_issued − float_spent`. This is money handed over that has not yet been spent.
- Beneath it in small type: total issued all-time · total spent all-time · date and amount of the last issuance
- If the figure is **negative**, he spent more than he was given, so the business owes him. Show it in red with the label "You owe him Rs X" rather than a minus sign, which is easy to misread.
- Also show his role, assigned projects, active/inactive status, and last login

#### Cash ledger (the main content)

A single chronological list, newest first, that reads like a passbook — not two separate tables the owner has to reconcile in his head:

- Cash issued to him → **+** amount (no project attached — see §4.7)
- Each expense he paid from the float → **−** amount, with expense name, project and category
- Cash returned → **−** amount
- A **running balance** column on every row, so the owner can see exactly when the float went up and down
- Filterable by date range and by project; a project filter also shows how much of his float went to each site

#### Actions on this page (admin only)

- **Issue cash** — opens the §4.7 form pre-filled with this user
- **Record cash returned** — a negative issuance
- **Adjustment** — for when the physical cash count does not match the app (a forgotten entry, a rounding difference). Requires a mandatory note, is clearly marked as an adjustment in the ledger, and is written to the audit log like everything else. Never let an admin silently overwrite the balance; the correction must be visible as its own row.
- Assign or unassign projects · Reset password · Deactivate

> **What does not touch the float.** Only expenses marked *Paid via: Site float* draw the balance down. An expense the admin paid himself (*Paid via: Direct*) never affects anyone's float, even if a site manager typed it in. Get this wrong and the balance will drift from the real cash in his pocket within days, which defeats the whole feature.

The site manager sees a read-only version of his own page — header and ledger, no actions, and nothing about any other user.

### 6.13 Admin settings

Users and invitations, role assignment, password resets, deactivation, project assignments (all per §2.1–2.3), category management (rename / merge), vendor merge, edit-window setting, Trash & Restore, audit log viewer.

### 6.14 Search and filtering

These are not a polish item. With dozens of projects and tens of thousands of expense rows, an unsearchable list is a dead list. Build search and filters into every screen that shows more than about ten rows.

#### Global search

- A persistent search box in the top bar, reachable from anywhere
- Searches across projects (name, client name, address), expenses (name, purpose, category, payee), vendors, and users, with results grouped by type
- Partial matching from anywhere in the word, not just the start — typing `ment` finds "Cement"
- Case-insensitive and punctuation-tolerant, so Roman Urdu spelling variations still match (`mistri`, `Mistri`, `mistry` should all find each other via trigram similarity)
- Debounced at roughly 300 ms; results appear as you type
- **Search obeys permissions.** A site manager's results are silently restricted to his own assigned projects and his own float — never a "no permission" message that reveals something exists. Implement through the same RLS-backed views as everything else, not by filtering in the browser.

#### Filters on list screens

| Screen              | Filters required                                                                                                                                                                                                         |
|---------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| Expenses            | Date range (presets: Today · Yesterday · This week · This month · Last month · This year · Last 12 months · Custom) · project · category · payee · paid or unpaid · paid via float or direct · entered by · amount range |
| Projects            | Status (active / on hold / completed / archived) · project type · assigned user · free-text on project or client name · sort by balance due, spend, margin or start date                                                 |
| Client payments     | Date range · project · method · linked milestone                                                                                                                                                                         |
| Payables            | Payee · project · age of the unpaid bill · amount range                                                                                                                                                                  |
| Cash ledger (§6.12) | Date range · project · type (issued / spent / returned / adjustment)                                                                                                                                                     |
| Users               | Role · active or inactive · assigned project                                                                                                                                                                             |
| Trash               | Record type · project · who deleted it · deletion date range                                                                                                                                                             |

#### Behaviour rules

- Filters combine — each additional filter narrows the result, never replaces the previous one
- Active filters show as removable chips above the list, so it is always obvious why rows are missing. A user staring at an empty screen because of a filter he forgot is a common and avoidable support problem.
- **Totals respect the filters.** If the list is filtered to cement in September on one project, the total shown is that total — not the unfiltered figure. State this plainly to the developer; getting it wrong produces numbers that look authoritative and are wrong.
- Filter state persists in the URL, so a filtered view can be bookmarked or shared, and survives a page refresh
- A one-tap Clear all
- Every filtered list can be exported to CSV exactly as filtered (Phase 2)
- Server-side filtering and pagination throughout — never fetch every row and filter in the browser

### 6.15 Tooltips and in-app guidance

Several figures in this app are easy to confuse with one another — money received versus money in hand, own money versus client money, float versus spend. Every computed figure must explain itself on the spot, so nobody has to guess or ask.

- A small **?** icon beside every card, column header and calculated total. Tap or hover shows one or two plain sentences.
- Written in the same basic English as the rest of the app (§9). No accounting vocabulary. Say what the number means and, where useful, how it is worked out.
- Tooltips are content, not code — keep them in one strings file so wording can be fixed without touching components
- Form fields whose meaning is not self-evident get helper text permanently under the input, not hidden behind an icon — *Paid via*, *Project type*, and the quantity/rate behaviour
- Empty states explain the screen rather than showing a blank box: "No expenses yet. Tap Add Expense to record the first one."
- A short first-run walkthrough on the site manager's dashboard the first time he logs in, three or four steps, skippable and repeatable from the menu

#### Required tooltip copy (use close to verbatim)

| Figure                       | Tooltip                                                                                                                   |
|------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| Client has paid              | Total money this client has sent you for this project so far.                                                             |
| Client still owes            | The agreed project price minus what the client has paid so far.                                                           |
| Total spent                  | Everything recorded as an expense on this project, whether you have paid the bill yet or not.                             |
