## 7. Data safety — no hard deletes, and a second copy

This is a hard requirement, not a nice-to-have. Four layers:

#### Layer 1 — Soft delete everywhere, with a visible Trash

Every table carries `deleted_at timestamptz` and `deleted_by uuid`. "Delete" in the UI sets these fields. No code path issues a SQL `DELETE` on business tables. All default queries and views filter `deleted_at IS NULL`.

Deleted records are not merely retained in the database — they are **visible to the admin as a second set of records** in a dedicated **Trash** screen, so he can always see exactly what has been removed and put it back.

- Trash lists every soft-deleted record across all types — expenses, client payments, projects, milestones, vendors — with tabs or a type filter
- Each row shows: what it was (name and amount), which project it belonged to, its original date, **who deleted it**, and **when it was deleted**
- Searchable and filterable by type, project, date range and deleting user
- **Restore** button on every row returns the record to normal use, recalculating all affected project totals immediately. Restoring a deleted project restores its children with it.
- Bulk restore for multiple selected rows
- Nothing auto-expires from Trash. There is no "empty trash" action and no permanent-delete button anywhere in the application.
- Trash is admin-only. Site managers cannot delete, so they never see it.

Deleting a parent record cascades softly: soft-deleting a project marks its expenses, payments and milestones deleted too, tagged with the same cascade reference so a restore brings back exactly the same set.

#### Layer 2 — Audit log as the mirror copy

A single `audit_log` table, written by Postgres triggers on every INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE across all business tables, storing the complete row as JSONB before and after. Because the whole row is captured on every change, every historical version of every record is recoverable from this one table — this is the duplicate data store. It is **append-only**: no role, including admin, has UPDATE or DELETE rights on it.

#### Layer 3 — Point-in-time recovery (deferred)

The project starts on **Supabase's free tier**, which does not include point-in-time recovery. This is an accepted decision: protection rests on layers 1, 2 and 4, which are all built in the application itself and work on any plan. Upgrading to Pro later enables PITR with no code change, and is worth revisiting once real financial history has accumulated.

> **Two free-tier limits to design around.** (1) Free Supabase projects **pause after a week of inactivity** — harmless here since the app is used daily, but if a quiet period is expected, a scheduled ping keeps it awake. (2) Scheduled functions for the daily export must be checked against free-tier cron availability; if unavailable, run the export from an external free scheduler hitting a secured endpoint.

#### Layer 4 — Off-database daily snapshot

A scheduled Edge Function (daily at 02:00 PKT) exports every table to JSON/CSV into a separate Storage bucket, filename stamped with the date, retained 30 days, and mirrored to Google Drive. With PITR deferred, **this is the primary defence against database-level loss** and should not be treated as optional. Verify the first few exports by hand before trusting it.

