## 15. Confirmed decisions

These two points were raised and have been settled by the owner. Build to them.

#### Salaries are project expenses

Staff salaries are charged to the project they relate to, as ordinary expenses under the **Staff salary** category. There is no separate company-overhead ledger and no cost-allocation logic in v1. When one person's salary spans two sites, the owner splits the amount manually across two expense entries. Build nothing special for this.

#### The project is the unit of account — there is no client record

Client name and phone stay as plain fields on the project. **Do not build a clients table**, client pages, or any cross-project client totals. If one person has two houses running, they are two independent projects with separate contract values, payments and balances, and the app makes no attempt to link them. Every figure in §5 is scoped to a single project.

Should repeat clients become common later, this is the change to make: promote client to its own table, migrate the existing name fields into records, and add a client view summing lifetime paid and total outstanding across their projects. Nothing in the current schema blocks that.

#### Advances to labour are plain expenses

An advance paid to a mistri or contractor is recorded as a normal expense on the date the money leaves, under **Advance to labour**. There is no drawdown ledger and no linking of an advance to a later settlement.

> **Consequence to guard against — double counting.** Because advances are plain expenses, when the contractor is finally settled the user must record *only the remaining balance*, not the full contract amount, or the site will appear to have spent the advance twice. Mitigate in the UI: when a user adds an expense with a payee who has earlier entries in the **Advance to labour** category on the same project, show a non-blocking hint listing those advances and their total — "Rs 1,50,000 in advances already recorded for this payee". Do not block the save; the user decides.

